+# A3 bounded paired compaction prototype
+
+`pto.tcompact.compare` selects FP32 values and their INT32 column indices with
+one scalar comparison mask. It is an explicit DPS operation with values, indices
+and exact per-row counts as results, plus a separate write/read scratch operand.
+This is a local A3 prototype, not a claim of upstream or A5 support.
+
+Supported predicates are `eq`, `gt` and `ge`. Source physical columns must be a
+multiple of 64; valid rows and columns may vary at runtime. Values and indices
+have equal, 32-byte-aligned per-row capacities no larger than the source columns.
+Count storage is `[1, align_up(source_rows, 8)]` INT32. Scratch needs
+`4*C + max(1024, align_up(C,256)/8 + 4*C)` bytes, where `C` is physical columns.
+
+For each row, only the first `min(count, capacity)` outputs are defined. Count
+always reports the full number of matches. Zero matches write only count, and
+overflow cannot write beyond output capacity. Selected indices are
+`offset + original_column` using INT32 arithmetic; callers must ensure the sum
+fits INT32. Selected FP32 values are moved as 32-bit data, without conversion.
+Source order and the selected values' bit representations are retained.
+
+The implementation constructs integer indices on Vector, compares source values,
+and reuses the mask for two `vreducev2` operations. Bounded copies publish results.
+The compiler models Vector output writes, Scalar count writes and scratch effects.
+There is no hidden output in scratch and no global memory or host synchronization.
+
+The consumer decides whether compaction was worthwhile. It must not read undefined
+tails as valid values, and must preserve its original sorting tie policy. The
+additional value reduction and output copy may outweigh removed random gathers;
+whole-kernel paired measurements are required before adopting an optimization.
